<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of Jenkins' Ear</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of Jenkins' Ear was a conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> fought mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and on the American mainland from 1739. It took its name from the ear of Robert Jenkins, a British captain mutilated by Spanish coastguards in 1731. After a British failure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a> in 1741, it merged into the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> and ended with the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1748.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear.jpg" alt="War of Jenkins' Ear" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Overton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_new_and_correct_map_of_the_trading_part_of_the_West_Indies.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
